{% set local_problem %}
<p>Lakefront lots slope toward the lake — that's what makes the views spectacular. But that same slope creates drainage challenges that surface solutions can't fully address.</p>

<p>Surface downspout extensions deposit water a few feet from your foundation. On a slope, that water follows gravity: either back toward your house or down the hillside, causing erosion. Neither outcome is acceptable for lakefront property.</p>

<p>Erosion threatens both your home and your investment. Losing soil on a lakefront lot means losing property value. Foundation movement from erosion or moisture causes expensive structural damage. And in Guntersville's humid environment, any water near your home accelerates rot and corrosion.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve these problems by transporting water through buried pipes to safe discharge points. No surface runoff, no erosion, no moisture accumulating near your foundation.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water away through sealed channels. Water flows by gravity through 4-inch PVC or corrugated pipe to a discharge point at the property edge or in a stable drainage area.</p>

<p>For lakefront properties, we design systems that account for slopes, soil conditions, and optimal discharge locations. The goal is getting water far enough from your home that it can disperse safely — without causing erosion or affecting neighboring properties.</p>

<p>The system works automatically with no moving parts, no electricity, and no maintenance. Water simply disappears from your downspouts and emerges at the discharge point, protecting your home around the clock whether you're there or not.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been installing underground drainage systems on Lake Guntersville properties for over 20 years. We understand lakefront conditions: the slopes, the soil, the humidity, and the importance of protecting waterfront investments.</p>

<p>Underground drains are a significant investment, but for many lake homes, they're the only solution that actually works. We'll assess your property honestly and tell you whether you need this level of protection.</p>
{% endset %}
